# Line endings are pinned rather than left to each contributor's
# core.autocrlf, because two of these files break silently when they are wrong.

# gradlew is a shell script. A CRLF in the repository copy fails on Linux and macOS
# with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" — an error that points at the shell, not at
# line endings, and that only appears on someone else's machine.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Windows batch files require CRLF. The reverse mistake is just as broken.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never line-ending-normalise a binary. Git's content sniffing usually catches a
# jar, but a single stray CRLF in a jar breaks its signature check and produces a
# "corrupt" wrapper that cannot be diagnosed from the symptom.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.so binary

# Source and configuration: LF in the repository, whatever the platform checks
# out. Nothing here has a CRLF requirement, and a normalised repository keeps
# diffs free of whole-file churn when contributors have mixed settings.
*.dart text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.kts text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.gradle text eol=lf
